answer:
The pre-approved purposes for the recognised legitimate interest basis include:

- Safeguarding "vulnerable" people;
- Responding to emergencies;
- Preventing or investigating crime;
- National security, public security and defense;
- Sharing personal information with an organization that needs it for their public task or function at their request.

These purposes cannot apply if you're a public authority processing personal information to perform your official tasks.

When processing special category data, in addition to having a lawful basis, you must also identify a condition for using this type of information.

This requirement is explicitly stated in the relevant provision in the UK GDPR, article 9, which states:

"Special categories of data shall be processed only where one of the following grounds exists:

(a) The data subject has given his or her consent;

(b) Processing is necessary for the performance of a contract to which the data subject is a party or in order to take steps at the request of the data subject prior to entering into a contract;

(c) Processing is necessary for compliance with a legal obligation to which the controller is subject;

(d) Processing is necessary for the performance of a task carried out in the public interest or in the exercise of official authority vested in the controller;

(e) Processing is necessary for reasons of substantial public interest;

(f) Processing is necessary for the protection of the vital interests of the data subject or of another natural person;

(g) Processing is necessary for the performance of a task carried out in the public interest or in the exercise of official authority vested in the controller."

The source [2] provides further details on how to document these conditions and demonstrates compliance with the UK GDPR.
